\lispPackageChapter{livermore/xcs-ternary-predicate}

The \texttt{livermore/xcs-ternary-predicate} package is the classical XCS
allele: true, false, or don't-care.  A condition is a sequence of these
predicates, one per bit of the situation.

\section{Types}

\lispTypeSubsection{ternary-value}

The type of a ternary allele: \texttt{t}, \texttt{nil}, or \texttt{:\#}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ternary-value?}

True when its argument is one of those three values.

\section{Classes}

\lispClassSubsection{ternary-predicate}

A predicate whose \texttt{value} slot is a \texttt{ternary-value}.  The
don't-care value \texttt{:\#} matches both \texttt{t} and \texttt{nil}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ternary-predicate}

A constructor.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(ternary-predicate \lispVar{value})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(ternary-predicate t)
(ternary-predicate :#)
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Methods}

\texttt{covering?} is true only for \texttt{:\#}.  \texttt{covering-score} is
$1$ or $0$ accordingly.

\texttt{match?} against a situation bit is true if the predicate is
\texttt{:\#} or if the two boolean senses agree.  \texttt{match?} against
another predicate is true if this predicate matches every situation the other
matches.

\texttt{more-general?} is true only when the general predicate is
\texttt{:\#} and the specific one is not.

\texttt{cover} maps over a situation sequence and, with probability
$P_{\#}$, writes \texttt{:\#} instead of the situation bit.

\texttt{mutate} flips between \texttt{:\#} and the current situation bit
with probability $\mu$.
